\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Final illumination configurations selected for the dual-illumination system: (a)~BY yellow backlight for grain boundary segmentation, (b)~WB white top-light for general classification, and (c)~BR red backlight for multispectral defect classification.}}{16}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Prototype~I: low-fidelity 3D-printed enclosure validating the dual-illumination hypothesis.}}{18}{figure.caption.37}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Prototype~II at industrial scale, showing the high-density occlusion problem that redirected the subsequent design brief.}}{19}{figure.caption.39}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces The 100-gram grain sample before and after a single solenoid actuation cycle, demonstrating the transition from dense occlusion to measurable grain isolation and orientation diversity.}}{24}{figure.caption.46}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Timing diagram for the staggered asynchronous solenoid pulse protocol. Each solenoid ($S_A$ through $S_D$) activates sequentially with a 50\,ms inter-solenoid delay and remains active for 500\,ms. The complete four-solenoid cycle spans 650\,ms. The staggered pattern produces a travelling impulse wave across the tray, generating grain rotation and dispersion without interference between simultaneous activations.}}{26}{figure.caption.49}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Physical assembly sequence of the final prototype. (a)~Custom PCB for solenoid pulse timing. (b--d)~Progressive chassis and electronics assembly. (e)~Completed device prepared for deployment.}}{29}{figure.caption.54}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces CAD exploded view of the Drishti device, showing the vertical component stack: optics module, electromagnetic actuation array, and control electronics within the aluminium chassis.}}{30}{figure.caption.53}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Drishti system architecture: the Raspberry Pi 5 edge node handles physical actuation and image capture; the central inference server handles segmentation and returns per-category $W/W\%$ results.}}{34}{figure.caption.60}%
